    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you go a bit deeper into the actual mechanism? like, how does the leucine specifically interact with the cell to flip that mTOR switch? i want to be able to explain the cellular signaling part more clearly to the client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine primarily interacts with the Rag GTPases, which act as critical upstream activators of mTORC1. When leucine levels are high, it facilitates the activation of these Rag GTPases, allowing mTORC1 to move from an inactive to an active state. This activation phosphorylates key downstream targets, such as S6K1 and 4E-BP1, which ultimately permits increased translation of mRNA into new muscle proteins. Essentially, leucine acts as the molecular \"key\" that unlocks the mTORC1 machinery.",
      "source": "target",
